---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import UpdateGenerator from '../../components/UpdateGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="SQL UPDATE Statement Generator" description="Generate a properly formatted UPDATE statement from a table, fields to change, and a WHERE condition.">
  <UpdateGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why UPDATE shares DELETE's dangerous missing-WHERE risk</h2>
  <p>Just like DELETE, an UPDATE statement without a WHERE clause modifies every row in the table — a mistake that's just as damaging as an accidental mass delete, since it can silently overwrite data across the entire table with the wrong value.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter the table and the fields/values to update.</li>
    <li>Specify the WHERE condition identifying exactly which rows to change.</li>
    <li>Review carefully before executing, especially against production.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p><code>UPDATE users SET status = 'inactive' WHERE last_login &lt; '2023-01-01';</code></p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Running UPDATE without WHERE</strong>, silently overwriting every row in the table with the same value.</li>
    <li><strong>Not testing the WHERE clause as a SELECT first</strong> to confirm it matches exactly the intended rows before switching to UPDATE.</li>
    <li><strong>Updating without a transaction on a production database</strong>, removing the ability to roll back if the result turns out wrong.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Bulk-updating a status or flag column based on a specific condition</li>
    <li>Correcting a data entry error affecting a known set of records</li>
    <li>Writing a data migration script that transforms existing data</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>How can I safely test an UPDATE before running it?</strong> Run the same WHERE clause as a SELECT first to confirm exactly which rows would be affected, and consider wrapping the UPDATE in a transaction you can roll back if needed.</p>
</ToolLayout>
